WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.778
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.079 --> 00:00:06.620
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN:
I'm with Firebase

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.840
and I've been at
Google for two years.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.160
And this is my third
time being at Next.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.240
I remember the first
time I was there.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.820
It was almost two years ago,
just a few blocks from here.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.190
I see someone
nodding over there.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.960
It was in the Warfield
Theater at Market Street.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.380
Who else was there?

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:25.510
OK, just a few people.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.340
And actually,
that's how it feels.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.100
To me, that was my first
big Google conference,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.350
and I was really, to
be honest, intimidated

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.240
with the number of people there.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.750
And now it's two years later
and there's over 10,000 of us

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.550
here at Moscone.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.650
It's grown so much.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.710
As we say at Google, it's
growing at the speed of clouds.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:55.140
I remember when we launched
App Engine back in 2008.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.540
It was the first tool
of what would later

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.640
become Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.280
But to be honest, for us
it was just a new thing

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.260
that we wanted to
release out there.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.880
And now, less than 10 years
later, we have over 150 APIs

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.340
that you can use in the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.980
It's grown at a tremendous rate.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.530
That's a thing that you
constantly see in technology.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.990
Not only do things
keep getting better,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:23.180
they get better
faster and faster.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.260
I'd like to take you back on a
bit of a journey back in time.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.830
So we founded Google
almost 20 years ago.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.830
And our mission was to organize
the world's information

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.940
and to make it universally
accessible and useful.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.610
And that's still
our mission today,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.350
but that's pretty
much the only thing

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.240
that has remained constant.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.600
The technology that we have
available to reach that mission

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.821
has grown so much
since 20 years ago.

00:01:49.821 --> 00:01:50.320
Think of it.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.840
It was essentially the early
days of the public internet.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.280
Since we're traveling
back in time,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.950
I always think back of
when I got my first PC.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.740
It was just under 30 years ago.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.130
And it was sort of a hideous
box that you would rather

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.630
put on the far corner of your
desk, or even better, under it.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.480
But it was my personal computer.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.026
It had a whopping 20
megabyte hard drive.

00:02:16.026 --> 00:02:17.650
And that made me one
of the lucky ones,

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.010
because hard drives were not
very common 30 years ago.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.310
It had, let's see, one
megabyte of memory.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.620
And that was a lot back then.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.360
But if you now look at
your phone or your laptop,

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.000
you probably have eight gigs or
maybe even 16 gigs of memory.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.440
So that's 8,000 times
or 16,000 times more

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.600
in less than 30 years.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.340
Moore's law has
been working hard

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.080
for us in the past decades.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.370
And my PC ran at 60
megahertz, and that was a lot.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.250
And it made me feel
like a magician,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.380
because here was a
computer on my desk

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.920
that I could make do
whatever I wanted.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.690
I came up with a
thing in my mind,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.730
and I would translate
it into codes,

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.910
and then the PC would do
exactly what I wanted.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.710
Or maybe not really
exactly what I wanted,

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:08.250
it mostly would do
exactly what I told it,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.490
but still it was pretty good.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:17.070
And it's a magical moment,
because no generation

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.560
before ours had
wielded so much power

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.840
over such powerful technology.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.070
It was like magic.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.210
That brings me to one
of my favorite quotes,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.410
which is, "Any sufficiently
advanced technology is

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.140
indistinguishable from magic."

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.550
It's from a science fiction
author, Arthur C. Clarke.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.370
And it ties two of my
favorite topics together.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.860
Technology-- that's
why I'm here on stage--

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.570
and science fiction, because
like many geeks, I love sci-fi.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.290
I love sci-fi for many reasons.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.160
They are often inspired
stories, but also they

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.640
seem to inspire
technology quite often.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.910
For example, if you think
back of the original Star Trek

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:03.560
series, it was launched in
the mid '60s on network TV.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.290
And the crew had to
communicate with each other.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:10.630
And they did that using devices
that they called communicators.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:16.700
And this is 50 years
ago, so clearly that

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.290
didn't exist back then.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.532
So the writers of this
show were in a room

00:04:20.532 --> 00:04:22.990
and they had to make up the
thing that would allow the crew

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.180
to communicate.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.110
So they wrote in the script,
they're using communicators.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:30.350
And then the props team would
look at that and is like OK.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.110
So we take a piece of
wood, and we paint it,

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.360
and that's how we communicate.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.690
The technology
didn't exist then.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.180
But if you fast
forward 30 years,

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.170
you see that actually the mobile
phones became commonplace.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.940
And people across
the entire world

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.825
could communicate with
their mobile phones.

00:04:48.825 --> 00:04:50.200
And not surprisingly,
if you look

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.810
at the first mobile phones,
they looked somewhat

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.590
like Star Trek communicators.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.430
Like if you have ever
had a Motorola StarTAC,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.440
it was clearly the
same type of design.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.975
You can see how technology
takes inspiration

00:05:02.975 --> 00:05:03.850
from science fiction.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.780
I always had to
laugh at the Picard.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:13.180
If he wanted a drink, he would
say, "Computer, Tea, Earl Grey,

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.040
Hot."

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.010
And it would materialize there.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.350
He probably said it with a
slightly more British accent

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:22.040
than I can put up here.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.950
Now we don't have the
materialization technology yet,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.330
not even us at Google.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:29.640
But I'm quite sure somebody
somewhere is working on that.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.430
But voice recognition?

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.030
I think we've done a bit better
than they did in the series,

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:38.540
because if you now use your
Google Home or your Assistant,

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.220
you can talk to it
and give instructions

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.100
that are a lot more complex
than what Picard used to do.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.847
And it will understand
you perfectly.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.730
But one thing about sci-fi I
always find most fascinating

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:54.680
is when they would send
the team to the planet.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.800
And they roughly had three types
of scenarios that could happen.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.767
The first scenario is that the
planet was completely empty.

00:06:01.767 --> 00:06:03.600
And that typically meant
that the planet was

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.910
about to explode, or
the sun in the system

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.120
was about to go supernova
and they had about 40 minutes

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.750
to get out of there.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:13.760
Second option was that
the planet was inhabited

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.980
by non-humanoid creatures.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.350
And those non-humanoid
creatures would then not

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.320
speak any language that the
crew could have comprehended.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:25.100
So the entire episode was about
finding a way to communicate.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.320
And those were some of
my favorite episodes,

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.280
because I love
communication problems.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.110
But the third type of episode
and by far the most common one

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.450
was that they would
beam down to the planet,

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.647
they would find
humanoid creatures,

00:06:38.647 --> 00:06:40.230
and those humanoid
creatures would all

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.170
speak perfect English.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:44.790
I'm not sure.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.320
But I don't fully
get how that works,

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.450
because I live on
one planet, and not

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.250
everyone speaks English.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.770
How do we have a
galaxy that's filled

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:57.670
with many different races and
they speak perfect English?

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.440
I don't think it's realistic.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:01.720
But what annoyed
me is, they also

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.220
never gave an explanation
for how this works.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.550
And language, for me,
is a fascinating topic.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.980
Because I often think
that it's the next barrier

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:12.438
that we're trying
to break through.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.754
For example, if you
have Google Translate,

00:07:16.754 --> 00:07:18.170
you travel to a
country where they

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:21.350
speak a different language,
you see something on the menu,

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.980
you take out your phone, you
type the word on the menu,

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.450
and you see what
you want to order.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:29.360
Or maybe if there's a sign,
you point your camera to it,

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.830
Google Translate
will show the sign

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.750
in the language you understand.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:36.130
And that's technology we've been
working at for a few decades.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.310
It's pretty good.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:41.570
But about two or
three years ago,

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:44.380
I saw that there were
releases of video conferencing

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:48.130
software that would do
live translation of speech.

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:50.110
So there was a
video release where

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:53.530
you saw a class of
school kids in the US.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.600
They were sitting
in front of a PC.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.250
And on the other
end of the PC, there

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.583
was a class of school kids
somewhere else in the world--

00:07:59.583 --> 00:08:01.360
let's say in Spain--

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.600
and the kids in the US would
say something in English.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:09.340
And the kids in Spain would hear
that same sentence in Spanish.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:11.119
And the kids in
Spain would respond,

00:08:11.119 --> 00:08:13.660
and the English kids would hear
the same sentence in English.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.810
And I was like, this is magic.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:20.072
This is the future
of communication.

00:08:20.072 --> 00:08:22.980
But as I just said,
if it's like magic,

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.820
then it's probably just a
very advanced technology.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.349
I also thought, wait.

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:29.640
I didn't see this in Star Trek.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:30.598
But I saw this earlier.

00:08:30.598 --> 00:08:32.460
I read about it somewhere.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.370
Because like any good geek,
I read The Hitchhiker's Guide

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.679
to the Galaxy by Douglas Adams.

00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:39.900
And he had a good
explanation of why everyone

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.309
could talk to each other.

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:43.610
Because he said,
everyone, at birth,

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:47.330
is implanted in their ear with
a tiny creature called a Babel

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.450
fish.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:51.590
And a Babel fish will take
whatever language is coming in.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.960
It will make it into the
language that they understand.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.580
That's how everyone
could communicate

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:58.300
in the entire galaxy.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.780
I'm like, ah, this
is Babel fish.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.233
I get it.

00:09:03.233 --> 00:09:06.060
But it's been two
and a half years,

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.307
and I still don't have
this implanted in my ear.

00:09:08.307 --> 00:09:10.140
And now if you saw the
keynote this morning,

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.800
Urs had a tiny chip.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.630
But I didn't get access
to that one in time.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:17.740
So what I think
is realistic today

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:20.080
is that we actually
turn this into an app.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.060
So I want to have
an app on my phone

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.330
where I have a dial that
makes me select the language.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.480
And that's the language that
I speak and that I understand.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.589
And then somebody else, say
in Japan, also has that app.

00:09:31.589 --> 00:09:33.880
And they select their language,
and that's the language

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.910
that they will hear that
they will speak to me.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:39.370
This is sort of what they
did in the school class,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.710
and what Douglas Adams
predicted, but then something

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:44.760
that we could do
today, probably.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.970
And when I say today
I really mean today.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:51.130
Because I'm checking
the counter here--

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.770
we have a good half hour left.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:54.670
We are Cloud Next.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.790
I just said that we're going
to pick the pace up a bit.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.180
So I think we can totally build
this app today here on stage.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.600
Thing is, I say we,
because I definitely

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.060
know that I can't build
this one on my own.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.190
So that's why I invited
a few of my friends

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:10.690
to help me out here on stage.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.962
Mike, Jen, let's build an app.

00:10:12.962 --> 00:10:19.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:20.924
MIKE MCDONALD: Thanks, Frank.

00:10:20.924 --> 00:10:23.960
Does anyone here have a
boss who stands on stage

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.732
and pitches for 15
minutes this magical idea,

00:10:26.732 --> 00:10:28.940
and then hands you a microphone
and says, go build it

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:29.900
in the next half hour?

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:30.899
I see a couple of hands.

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:31.430
Yeah.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.280
That's what we're
doing right now.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.230
So if you didn't come
to this presentation,

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:40.830
you might start thinking, this
is how we need to build an app.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.440
We have our mobile
applications that

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:45.290
make RESTful calls to
some application server.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:49.760
So that could be
Java or PHP or Ruby.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.770
And then that application
server handles

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.082
all of the complex
application logic.

00:10:54.082 --> 00:10:55.790
That's going to handle
our authentication

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:56.930
and authorization.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:59.090
That's going to handle
all the translation, all

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.550
of the storing of the files.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.040
And it's going to ship it
out to the other cloud API,

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.860
so the cloud databases
and cloud storage examples

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.140
that store all of that.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:12.680
But the counter says 46
minutes and 27 seconds.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.170
As easy as it is to
spin up VMs, that

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:16.490
would still take us too long.

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:19.195
We would be provisioning
all of our servers.

00:11:19.195 --> 00:11:21.320
Are we going to secure them
in the next 45 minutes?

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.028
Like, that's going to
be a really, really

00:11:23.028 --> 00:11:24.230
difficult problem.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:27.410
We need to move really
quickly to build this app

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:30.370
to meet Frank's specs.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.590
Luckily, you all
came to this talk.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:36.040
And you're going to learn the
secret to build applications

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:37.640
incredibly quickly.

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.400
And that secret
is using Firebase.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.470
With Firebase,
our mobile clients

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:46.010
talk directly to powerful,
managed infrastructure,

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:49.710
like a database and an
object storage solution.

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:52.100
This means that
your apps don't have

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:55.490
to go through that middle
tier and can speak directly

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:58.365
to those different APIs.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.480
In case you were asleep
during the keynote

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.370
and didn't know
what Firebase was,

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:06.990
Firebase is a full
application platform.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.420
It is Google's mobile platform.

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:11.300
It allows you to develop
your applications

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:15.030
on top of Google Cloud
Platform, grow your user base,

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:18.500
and monetize and
earn more money.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.060
Because this is a
develop talk, we're

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.280
going to focus just
on a couple of those

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:23.990
develop features
that will help build

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.200
our app as quickly as possible.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.280
First, a cloud storage solution
that lets you upload and share

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:33.530
exabytes-- literally exabytes--

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:35.750
of user data.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:39.860
Combine that with a serverless
authentication system that

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.890
handles complex authentication
flows and session management

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:44.510
with ease.

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:47.910
And lastly, a real time database
that stores and synchronizes

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:52.530
JSON data in milliseconds.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:54.720
Let's start first
with cloud storage.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.780
Frank said that he wants to talk
into his phone and upload that

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.770
file to share with everyone.

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:03.120
Cloud storage is Google's
way of storing up

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:04.710
to five terabyte objects.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:07.530
And obviously, our audio files
are not going to be that large.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.500
But it's good to have that kind
of scale behind the scenes.

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.800
We can use the Firebase
SDKs for cloud storage

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.140
to take a file that we
record on our device

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.270
and robustly upload
it to the cloud.

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:20.730
And by robust, how
many of you guys

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.450
are enjoying the
conference Wi-Fi?

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:23.996
It's a little flaky, right?

00:13:23.996 --> 00:13:25.120
What about mobile networks?

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:26.590
I commute in on a
train every day,

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:28.510
and I'm in a tunnel
for four minutes.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:29.750
I want to keep using my app.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:32.050
And if I start uploading,
I want to make sure

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.360
that that file
gets to the cloud.

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:38.470
The Firebase SDKs for cloud
storage handle that seamlessly.

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:44.050
We have SDKs for Android, iOS,
JavaScript, c++, and Unity.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.750
So we can meet your application
needs wherever they are.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:49.490
And it's all serverless.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.160
You don't have to spin up any
servers to proxy authentication

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.820
or get signed URLs
to upload from.

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:58.205
You can upload directly
to the cloud, securely.

00:13:58.205 --> 00:14:00.080
And when the time comes
to download and share

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:04.250
that content, it's all coming
over Google's incredibly

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:06.080
awesome world scale network.

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.090
I promise, things
download really quickly.

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:09.620
They're often edge cached.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:11.990
And it can instantly
be transmitted out

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.210
to hundreds, thousands,
or hundreds of thousands

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:17.010
of devices.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:18.770
But what about security?

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:20.270
Can anyone just upload a file?

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.760
We don't want-- we want
these two lovely folks

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.800
and we want all of you
to be able to upload

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:26.240
and download our files.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:29.690
But we don't want people outside
or people to upload nefarious

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:30.320
things.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:31.640
We need to secure that.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:35.250
And we can do that with
Firebase authentication.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.770
With Firebase authentication,
you can log in your users

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.440
using any of a number of
common social providers--

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:43.860
Google, Facebook, Twitter,
GitHub, your own custom email

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:46.020
password solution,
or we even have

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.200
integrations to do your
own custom authentication.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:51.300
So if you have SAML or
an existing SSO provider,

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.130
you can use that
directly with Firebase.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:56.400
Again, this is
completely serverless.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.240
You aren't spinning up
your own OAuth flows.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.167
Have people done OAuth flows?

00:15:00.167 --> 00:15:00.750
Couple people.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:01.696
Have people done SAML?

00:15:01.696 --> 00:15:02.820
That's also really painful.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:03.320
Yeah.

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:04.694
Bunch of people
raise their hand?

00:15:04.694 --> 00:15:05.250
Yeah.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.290
We handle all of those things.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:08.790
And it's really, really easy.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:12.870
No servers, no other three
legged OAuth flows or OAuth

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:13.890
redirects.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:18.080
You just use our SDKs directly
from those client libraries

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:20.860
and log your users in.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.740
The other really cool thing
about Firebase authentication

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.180
is under the hood we
federate all those users.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:29.350
So even if a user comes
in via Facebook or Google,

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.900
we can create a
single user account

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:33.970
and link across all of those.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:36.730
This is especially cool for
anonymous authentication.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:39.430
So if you have an
e-commerce site,

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:42.460
and you want users to add
items to their shopping cart,

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:45.260
you don't have to force
them to log in initially.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.800
But when they check out, and
you prompt them to log in,

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:50.590
you can seamlessly link
that anonymous account

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:52.810
with one of these
social providers,

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:54.760
and carry all of
their items over.

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:57.750
Again, without having to
prompt them to do this.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.990
It's really, really impressive.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:02.930
So now we're able
to upload our files

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.000
and download our files securely.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.670
But we need to synchronize
that file information

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:09.702
across all of our phones.

00:16:09.702 --> 00:16:11.910
And for that, we have the
Firebase Realtime Database.

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:15.430
The Firebase Realtime Database
was Firebase's first product.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:19.310
And it was kind of magical,
and you'll see why in a minute.

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:22.610
The Firebase Realtime Database
is a NoSQL JSON database.

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:24.860
It looks kind of
something like this.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:27.170
It's just a big blob
of JSON in the sky--

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:30.430
synchronizes across all
of your client devices.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:32.710
In our case, we're going
to be storing information

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:34.360
about uploads--

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:38.020
a language code, and
a link to the file

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.460
that's stored in cloud storage.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.220
Now when you use
traditional databases,

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:47.250
typically they follow a
request-response paradigm.

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.850
So I would have to pull
my database every five

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:55.140
seconds or every minute and say,
hey, is there a new audio file?

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.460
I'm listening for things.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:58.080
Please update me.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.637
The Firebase Realtime Database
doesn't work like that.

00:17:00.637 --> 00:17:02.220
We don't want your
mobile applications

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:05.700
pulling every five seconds and
burning through battery life.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:09.700
We want to tell you as a
developer and let you say,

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:12.839
hey, Firebase, let me
know when data changes.

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:16.170
And when any of those clients
makes a change in the database,

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:19.952
we can push that data out to all
of the devices in milliseconds.

00:17:19.952 --> 00:17:20.910
It's really impressive.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.201
You'll see it here in a minute.

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:26.099
But listening to me
talk is not going

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:27.690
to help Frank build his app.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:30.650
We need to switch
over to code and see

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:32.400
what it's like to
actually build this app.

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:38.580
Over on my right,
so everyone's left,

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:40.470
Frank is building an iOS app.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:43.320
And over on my left,
your right, Jenny

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.810
is building an Android app.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.520
And let's pop over
to the emulators

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:48.870
now that they're warm.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:51.900
And you can see the bare
structure of our application.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:55.200
We have a sign in, sign
out button up at the top.

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:56.910
We have a language
picker that lets

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.540
you select what language you
want to hear the recordings in.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.250
We have a big
"record" button that

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.310
lets you record and
then stop recording,

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:09.049
and a text field that
shows the translated text.

00:18:09.049 --> 00:18:11.590
And what's going to happen with
this application is once they

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:15.580
click "record", the app will get
an audio file on its local disk

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:18.160
that we can upload
to cloud storage.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.310
And then eventually, it
will get a notification

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:25.030
from the database that gives a
little bit of translated text

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:26.080
that will populate.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.060
So let's start filling
in the guts of that.

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:30.970
We've already added Firebase
through CocoaPods and Gradle

00:18:30.970 --> 00:18:33.530
to our apps and
configured it to be ready.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:35.200
So the first thing
that we want to do

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:37.720
is, within our application,
create instances

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:41.440
of storage, authentication, and
the Firebase Realtime Database.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:44.300
Super easy to go
ahead and do that.

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:47.620
The next thing that we're going
to do is start our file upload.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:49.900
So as I said, once you
push that record button,

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:53.630
we'll hand this function a file.

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:56.110
And we're going to create a
reference in cloud storage

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:58.210
where we want to
store that file.

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.491
We'll also add some
information about the file.

00:19:00.491 --> 00:19:02.740
In this case, we're going
to include the content type,

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:06.820
so the eventual APIs
know how to process that.

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:08.410
And then, using
one line of code,

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.150
we're going to upload
this to cloud storage.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:14.600
It is quite literally this easy.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:17.480
You just say what
file, hand it the file,

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:20.060
and we can upload it robustly.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:22.686
We also want to
know what happens

00:19:22.686 --> 00:19:23.810
when that file is uploaded.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:27.350
So we're going to add listeners
for failure and success.

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:29.210
On failure, we'll pop
up a little toast that

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:30.860
says, hey, the upload failed.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:32.830
You should probably
try something else.

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:34.910
And on success, we'll
write that metadata

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:38.680
to the real time database
so we can synchronize it.

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.900
You ready to try this out?

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:41.450
OK.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:43.783
Let's run the apps and
try uploading something.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.070
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Hello world.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:57.150
Famous first words.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:57.900
MIKE MCDONALD: Oh.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:19:59.065
Oh, what happened?

00:19:59.065 --> 00:20:00.992
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN:
Oh, did it work?

00:20:00.992 --> 00:20:02.310
MIKE MCDONALD: No, I
don't think it worked.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:03.810
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN:
Does yours work?

00:20:05.518 --> 00:20:08.330
JEN TONG: Hello world.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:09.840
MIKE MCDONALD: Aw, sad face.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.710
The upload failed.

00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:14.140
Well, you know why it failed?

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:17.290
Because we don't want to
allow anyone to upload files.

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:19.390
Cloud storage is
secured by default.

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:22.270
We need to add Firebase
authentication to authenticate

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:24.820
Frank and Jenny and
make sure that they only

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:27.100
are allowed to upload files.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:31.030
So, going back up to the top,
we have that Auth get instance.

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:33.610
We're going to first add a
little bit of business logic,

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.940
to check if we have a
user currently logged in.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.580
And after that, we're going
to cheat here a little,

00:20:39.580 --> 00:20:42.059
because we have 36
minutes remaining.

00:20:42.059 --> 00:20:44.350
Authentication is really
hard, and it's really critical

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:45.250
to get right.

00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:47.170
So Firebase has provided
a set of libraries

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:51.580
called Firebase UI that provide
whole social Auth flows.

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:54.790
So we can import that
library, set it up,

00:20:54.790 --> 00:20:57.790
and say that we want to
use email as well as Google

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:59.329
as authentication providers.

00:20:59.329 --> 00:21:01.120
And then what's going
to happen with that--

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:02.650
if the user is not
logged in, we'll

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:04.750
pop up that authentication
flow, and walk them

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:08.170
through a whole UI flow
to authenticate them.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:10.900
Otherwise, we'll start listening
for translations coming in

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.056
from the database.

00:21:13.056 --> 00:21:14.180
Let's try that again, guys.

00:21:20.394 --> 00:21:22.010
JEN TONG: Yes.

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:24.260
MIKE MCDONALD: There we go,
the screen just popped up,

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:27.900
Frank is going to click on
Sign In with Google, sign

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:32.410
in with his chosen demo Google
account, and here we are.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.780
Let's try uploading again.

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:35.470
- Hello world.

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:39.880
JEN TONG: Hello world.

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:44.490
MIKE MCDONALD: There we go.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:46.590
So we saw the toast
that said uploaded.

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:48.000
Let's go to the
Firebase console.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:51.030
And again, we don't want to
pull a cover over your eyes.

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.940
We want to show you that
file within our cloud storage

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:55.560
bucket.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.890
So here, you can see one file,
the audio AMR from Android

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:01.350
and the LINEAR16 file from iOS.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:03.690
Oh, that was pretty
magical, right?

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:05.220
Let's also hop
over the database,

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:06.970
just to make sure that
we're synchronizing

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:09.536
our metadata to the
Realtime Database as well.

00:22:09.536 --> 00:22:10.410
And there you can go.

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:13.890
You can see two files with
a bunch of file metadata

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:15.090
about Firebase.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:16.350
Again, so that was what?

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:17.562
15 lines of code?

00:22:17.562 --> 00:22:18.270
20 lines of code?

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:19.271
Something like that.

00:22:19.271 --> 00:22:19.770
Yeah.

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:20.770
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:21.270
Thank you.

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:25.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:26.150
OK.

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:27.680
So we have files uploading.

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:29.270
We're doing it securely.

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:32.720
Now we want to know any
time a new file is added.

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:34.764
We're going to listen
for a translation.

00:22:34.764 --> 00:22:37.430
And for that, we're going to use
the Firebase Realtime Database.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:39.970
So we're going to create another
reference to our database

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:42.940
and we're going to listen
for translations to come in.

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:45.760
Here, we're going to call
this a child added event that

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.520
says, any time a new
translation comes in, notify me

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:50.930
of that translation.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.300
And once we have
that translation,

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.760
we'll add yet another
listener to give us

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:57.600
the specific language code.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:00.830
So the way that we set it up, we
set it up as all the languages,

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:04.680
and then we pull
out a specific one.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.560
Once we have that
language code coming in,

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:09.990
we'll send that to the
native text to speech APIs

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:13.260
on Android and iOS to
play that translated text,

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:18.042
as well as populate the
translated text field below.

00:23:18.042 --> 00:23:19.500
Let's rerun the
app and try it out.

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:26.450
So Frank is going to come over.

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:30.320
And in our database, he's going
to simulate translating text

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:31.550
for us.

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:33.860
So we have a
translations reference,

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:39.470
he's going to add English
as the translation,

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:42.620
and say something
like hello world.

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:45.620
And if we're doing our
job here, hello world

00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:48.020
pops up there and
on the other app,

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:49.910
and it would speak
to us as well.

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:51.260
- Hello world.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:53.970
MIKE MCDONALD: There
you go, hello world.

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:55.170
So I'm done, right?

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:58.110
The app does exactly
what Frank wants, right?

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:00.650
He was standing there,
typing in the translations,

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:06.150
we could speak and he downloaded
and then typed them all out.

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:06.720
I don't know.

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:07.710
No?

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:11.100
OK, well, I'm done.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:13.762
I heard Google has some really
cool machine learning stuff.

00:24:13.762 --> 00:24:15.720
Did you guys see that in
the keynote yesterday?

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:16.560
Yeah?

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:17.880
So, Jenny.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.947
Do you think you can
help us out here?

00:24:20.947 --> 00:24:23.280
JEN TONG: You're not putting
me on the spot or anything,

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:23.942
are you?

00:24:23.942 --> 00:24:26.400
I kind of like the idea of
Frank doing all the translations

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:27.240
by hand in the back.

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:27.660
No?

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:29.070
MIKE MCDONALD: I kind
of do too, honestly.

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:29.611
JEN TONG: OK.

00:24:29.611 --> 00:24:32.101
OK, we'll actually write
some software to solve this.

00:24:32.101 --> 00:24:34.350
But before we do that, could
we go back to the slides,

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:35.730
and I'll tell you a
little bit about what

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:36.680
we're going to build.

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:43.321
Step through the coding slides.

00:24:43.321 --> 00:24:43.820
So yeah.

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:47.820
So we are going to be
using some software

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.200
to automate this part
of it, rather than have

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:54.120
Frank continue to code for us.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:57.154
And we're going to do that in
the trendiest way possible,

00:24:57.154 --> 00:24:58.820
because there's
probably a phrase you've

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:01.370
heard a few times during
this event so far,

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.310
which is machine learning.

00:25:03.310 --> 00:25:05.090
Specifically-- well,
rather generally,

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:07.460
we could build this
up from scratch.

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:08.992
But unfortunately,
that's something

00:25:08.992 --> 00:25:11.450
we're probably not going to be
able to do in the 20 minutes

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:13.220
we have left to write this app.

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:15.110
It takes a lot of time
to gather all the data

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:17.510
and train all the models
and get them all set up.

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.760
So we're going to take
a different approach.

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:22.490
We're going to use some of
those wonderful machine learning

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.971
backed APIs that are available
to solve a lot of problems.

00:25:25.971 --> 00:25:27.470
And there's a huge
list of them that

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:30.360
are part of Cloud Platform
that you can make use of.

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:32.810
And we're going to use a
couple specific ones to fill

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:37.090
in the gaps that Mike
left in our application.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:38.770
We're going to be
using the Cloud Speech

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:43.150
API to take the spoken word
from all the users of our apps

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.310
and convert that
into a transcription

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:48.200
and do a transcript, into text.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:49.940
Then once we have
that text, we're

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:53.240
going to use the Cloud
Translation API to translate it

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:56.570
into the other languages so
the other users of the app

00:25:56.570 --> 00:25:58.800
can hear it in
their chosen voice.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:04.590
So we have a solution
there, right?

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:07.650
We have our app up there
running against Firebase.

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:11.010
We're using the database
for all the structured data.

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:13.320
We have our audio
files in storage.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:17.140
And we have these cool Cloud
APIs to do the heavy lifting.

00:26:17.140 --> 00:26:19.230
We're done, right?

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:20.127
Well, kind of.

00:26:20.127 --> 00:26:22.710
There's one little line missing
from this diagram that kind of

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:25.260
messes it up a little bit,
which is how do we wire

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:29.050
all these things up together?

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:32.036
So if you were to come in
and solve this problem,

00:26:32.036 --> 00:26:33.910
using knowledge you
might have had before you

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:35.626
came to this talk--

00:26:35.626 --> 00:26:36.250
oh wait, sorry.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:38.583
Taking a step back, there are
a few different challenges

00:26:38.583 --> 00:26:42.310
that come up as our application
gets this additional level

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:43.900
of complexity.

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:46.840
And these are kind of an example
of larger problems that you

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:49.970
face as your mobile apps
get more complicated.

00:26:49.970 --> 00:26:52.510
So one of these
challenges is secrets.

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:54.250
So when we're
building mobile apps,

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:56.617
sometimes we have some
secret information.

00:26:56.617 --> 00:26:58.200
For example, if
you're writing a game,

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.790
you might have the
special code that

00:27:00.790 --> 00:27:02.320
helps you detect
when people cheat,

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:04.900
because people always cheat at
mobile games for some reason.

00:27:04.900 --> 00:27:06.910
Or if you're using
some Cloud APIs,

00:27:06.910 --> 00:27:09.930
like Speech and Translate,
you might have some API keys

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:14.480
that you wouldn't want somebody
using for their own purposes.

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:16.201
And the truth is, if
you take this code

00:27:16.201 --> 00:27:18.200
and you burn it into the
app and you release it,

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:21.290
somebody is going to find a way
to get that information out.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:24.750
And then you'll be sad,
because your API key will be

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.230
out of quota and you'll be sad.

00:27:27.230 --> 00:27:29.360
But there is a solution,
which is using the cloud.

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:32.026
We can take that information and
we can put it up into the cloud

00:27:32.026 --> 00:27:35.390
and get it out of the hands
of our meddling users.

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:38.500
And we're going to take
that path this time.

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:41.540
Another challenge you
face is resources.

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:44.740
So mobile phones are
amazingly powerful today.

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:46.480
They're as powerful
as supercomputers

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:48.910
from only a few years ago.

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:52.630
But there's one thing about them
that has not scaled with that.

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:54.370
It is batteries.

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:57.400
So you can do all sorts of
heavy lifting on that phone.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.590
But if the phone-- if your
user's battery is dead,

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:01.339
they're going to be
sad, and they will not

00:28:01.339 --> 00:28:03.100
be using your app very much.

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:05.260
So when you have really
resource intensive stuff,

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:08.299
you can take that load and
push it up into the cloud,

00:28:08.299 --> 00:28:10.090
and run it on more
powerful computers which

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:13.740
also have the luxury of being
plugged into mains electricity.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:16.480
Then your users will be happy,
because their phones will still

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:17.500
have batteries.

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:19.833
And they'll continue to use
your app and everything will

00:28:19.833 --> 00:28:21.800
be successful and happy.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:24.010
One more, which is
especially relevant to us,

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:28.700
as I can see the little clock
ticking down, is code sharing.

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:31.254
So one really big pain for
building a mobile application,

00:28:31.254 --> 00:28:33.170
especially if you want
to target a whole bunch

00:28:33.170 --> 00:28:35.210
of different
platforms, is having

00:28:35.210 --> 00:28:37.370
to rewrite the same
functionality over and over

00:28:37.370 --> 00:28:40.840
and over again for
different platforms.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:41.850
And this is a bummer.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:44.340
And there are a lot of
different approaches to trying

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:46.064
to solve this problem.

00:28:46.064 --> 00:28:47.730
But one approach to
solving this problem

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:50.187
is to put some of your
resource, some of your code,

00:28:50.187 --> 00:28:52.020
some of your business
logic up in the cloud,

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:54.210
and then connecting to
all of your mobile devices

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:56.010
over an API.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.800
Thereby only having to write
this logic once and share it

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:01.000
across all your
devices via that API.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:07.116
So if you were to try
to solve this problem,

00:29:07.116 --> 00:29:08.490
using maybe the
knowledge you had

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:10.800
before you came to
this talk, digging

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.300
into your traditional
bag of tricks,

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:14.410
you might come up with
a solution like this.

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:17.730
So we have our awesome
Firebase app running

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:18.860
on those mobile devices.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:20.580
We'll just spin up
some compute nodes,

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:22.470
and maybe we'll write some APIs.

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:25.944
We'll make a Django server or
we'll do some Node.js stuff.

00:29:25.944 --> 00:29:27.360
Then we'll stick
the keys in there

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.850
and use that to manage
all that stuff--

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:30.580
which is great.

00:29:30.580 --> 00:29:32.100
It'll be great when
you get started.

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.224
But then as your application
gets more use and more

00:29:34.224 --> 00:29:36.690
complexity, you're
going to find yourself

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:38.587
in a more complicated place.

00:29:38.587 --> 00:29:40.170
You're going to find
yourself managing

00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:43.380
all sorts of caches and firewall
rules and all sorts of stuff

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:45.849
like that.

00:29:45.849 --> 00:29:47.890
But we don't really want
to deal with that today.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:49.520
We want something fast and easy.

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:51.308
That's what this
talk is about, right?

00:29:51.308 --> 00:29:54.000
Yeah.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:57.090
And at Google, we've
been kind of helping

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:59.400
you take care of
your app management

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.189
woes for a long time.

00:30:01.189 --> 00:30:02.730
We want you to spend
the time writing

00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:04.500
the code for your
actual application

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:07.140
and spending less time
doing that other overhead.

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:12.000
One way I joke about it is, we
are code as a service hipsters.

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:13.702
Since 2008, when
App Engine launched,

00:30:13.702 --> 00:30:15.660
you've been able to push
a web app to the cloud

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:18.090
and not have to worry
about scaling up or down,

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:22.092
or how the cache is
implemented, that kind of stuff.

00:30:22.092 --> 00:30:24.300
And although App Engine is
still a wonderful solution

00:30:24.300 --> 00:30:27.060
to a lot of problems, it's
not quite a perfect fit

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:28.770
for what we're
dealing with today.

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:32.550
We want something a
little bit different.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:35.030
So that's why instead of
spitting up a bunch of servers

00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:37.070
on compute or using
App Engine, we're

00:30:37.070 --> 00:30:39.380
going to do something a
little bit different today.

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:42.960
We're going to use
Cloud functions.

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:43.460
Yay.

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:50.936
They are the perfect little
bit of application glue

00:30:50.936 --> 00:30:52.310
to hold our
application together.

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:57.980
And they will allow us to
solve all of those problems I

00:30:57.980 --> 00:30:58.770
mentioned before.

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:00.110
We're going to be
able to take our keys

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:01.693
and stuff them up
into those functions

00:31:01.693 --> 00:31:03.470
to keep everything
running nice and great

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:08.300
and away from the prying hands
of our less friendly users.

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:11.090
It would also allow us to
take the more heavy lifting

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.400
our application has to do,
in terms of the translation,

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:18.210
and take that off of the device
and put it into the cloud.

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:20.551
And I could tell you more
about how this works.

00:31:20.551 --> 00:31:22.050
But that's not what
you're here for.

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:24.930
You're here to see
some code, right?

00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:28.200
So let's build it.

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:30.420
So [INAUDIBLE], switch
it over to code view.

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:30.930
Awesome.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:32.970
So what we have
going on here is,

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:37.207
we have Frank, over here
on my right and your left,

00:31:37.207 --> 00:31:38.790
writing some code
and cloud functions.

00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:40.580
And we have the console
on the other side.

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:43.769
So you can see stuff
updating live in Firebase.

00:31:43.769 --> 00:31:45.810
So one of the first things
you're going to notice

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:48.720
is different than the last
chunk of live coding we did is--

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.750
cloud functions don't need a
lot of stuff to get started.

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:54.420
Previously, we had a couple
of lines of boilerplate code,

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:57.260
because mobile applications
are just kind of like that.

00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:59.010
But all we have for
cloud functions to get

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:02.400
started is some variables
up top, specifically

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:06.870
that specify some authentication
credentials for our cloud APIs,

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:09.330
and a function stub.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:12.812
And that's all we really
need to get started.

00:32:12.812 --> 00:32:14.520
So to get started,
what we're going to do

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:16.999
is we're going to
add another function.

00:32:16.999 --> 00:32:18.540
And what this function
is going to do

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:20.940
is it is going to
trigger whenever there

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.460
is a write to the database.

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:24.960
And the first thing
it's going to do

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:27.360
is it's going to extract
some information from some

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:30.630
of the metadata, including the
language the user was speaking,

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.130
as well as some
information that's

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:34.320
dependent on the phone
platform, like the sample

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:35.850
rate and the encoding.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.520
Which as it turns out is
different for each platform,

00:32:38.520 --> 00:32:41.134
we discovered while
we were writing this.

00:32:41.134 --> 00:32:42.300
So we're going to take that.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:43.180
We're going to hang on to it.

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:45.450
And then we're going to
send that information up

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:48.630
to the Cloud Speech API
along with a reference

00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:51.826
to the recorded file
from Firebase storage.

00:32:51.826 --> 00:32:53.700
And this is actually
one of my favorite parts

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:56.940
of this implementation, as you
can see that we don't actually

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.984
have to download the bits and
bytes of that audio recording.

00:32:59.984 --> 00:33:01.650
Because all this stuff
integrates really

00:33:01.650 --> 00:33:04.271
well together, we can just
send over a reference,

00:33:04.271 --> 00:33:05.520
which is nice and lightweight.

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:07.530
And then that API is just
going to slurp it down

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:12.080
from the same place that it
was stored by our mobile app.

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:14.760
And then once we get a response
back from the Cloud Speech API,

00:33:14.760 --> 00:33:16.218
we're going to take
that, and we're

00:33:16.218 --> 00:33:18.922
going to write it
back to the database.

00:33:18.922 --> 00:33:20.380
And this actually
kind of completes

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:23.230
one whole lap on our app.

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:28.040
We now have the point where our
app can take in that recording

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:31.150
and convert it into a
transcript that we should

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:33.700
be able to see in the database.

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:35.800
So should we give it a spin?

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:38.275
Let's give it a spin.

00:33:38.275 --> 00:33:39.400
MIKE MCDONALD: Hello world.

00:33:42.110 --> 00:33:43.860
JEN TONG: And I think
there it is already.

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:45.610
Oh, that's the file uploading.

00:33:45.610 --> 00:33:47.440
So we can see the
upload metadata.

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:49.300
So now everything
is going right.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:52.460
The function is chugging away.

00:33:52.460 --> 00:34:00.520
And in just a moment we will
see another node appear.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:01.880
And it will be awesome.

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:12.342
Sometimes it takes a moment
for the first one to spin up.

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:21.396
We're going to try
from the backup phone.

00:34:21.396 --> 00:34:22.980
Oh no, there it is.

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:24.620
We cannot blame the Wi-Fi.

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:25.409
Or maybe we can.

00:34:25.409 --> 00:34:26.075
But there it is.

00:34:26.075 --> 00:34:29.888
Hello world, in text,
in the database.

00:34:29.888 --> 00:34:30.929
MIKE MCDONALD: Can I try?

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:31.940
JEN TONG: Can you try?

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:34.239
Of course you can try,

00:34:34.239 --> 00:34:35.861
MIKE MCDONALD: Hello world.

00:34:35.861 --> 00:34:37.860
JEN TONG: I promise we're
going to say something

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:39.870
different at some point.

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:40.790
Why?

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:41.580
JEN TONG: Why?

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:42.795
There are other things
people want to communicate.

00:34:42.795 --> 00:34:44.066
MIKE MCDONALD: Oh, there it was.

00:34:44.066 --> 00:34:44.566
Right there.

00:34:44.566 --> 00:34:45.545
JEN TONG: Woo hoo.

00:34:45.545 --> 00:34:51.317
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:51.317 --> 00:34:53.150
But at this point, I've
only done about half

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:57.260
of what I promised I would do
when Mike handed me the baton.

00:34:57.260 --> 00:34:58.570
We've now taken the text--

00:34:58.570 --> 00:35:00.290
we've taken the
voice of the user

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:01.760
and we've converted into text.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:03.530
But it's only in
there in one language,

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.810
the language that
user spoke it in,

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:07.730
which is not quite right at all.

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:09.574
It's just kind of like
a chat device now.

00:35:09.574 --> 00:35:11.240
A communicator, we
want our communicator

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:13.940
to also have a
universal translator.

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:15.590
So we're going to
solve this problem

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:19.390
in the way you might expect,
by writing another function.

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:20.450
Yay.

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:21.950
So we're going to
go ahead and we're

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:26.090
going to add another function
in our cloud functions.

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:31.580
And this function is going to
trigger when that transcript is

00:35:31.580 --> 00:35:34.840
written to the database.

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:37.930
And it's going to get the
transcript text itself, as well

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:41.635
as the language that the
speaker was speaking.

00:35:41.635 --> 00:35:43.010
And then what it's
going to do is

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:45.680
it's going to iterate through
all of the different languages

00:35:45.680 --> 00:35:48.700
that our app supports--
seven, in this case.

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:50.630
And for each one of
those, it is going

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:54.260
to send that transcript along
with the source language

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:56.086
to the Translation API.

00:36:01.330 --> 00:36:04.590
And then as the responses come
back from the Translation API,

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:07.260
we're going to write those
back to a different node

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.450
in the database.

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:13.250
And then finally, once the last
translation has made it back,

00:36:13.250 --> 00:36:16.250
whichever translation decides
to be the slacker that time,

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.550
we're going to signal to the
function that we are done.

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:25.840
And this is one of the
anti-climactic parts

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.130
of Firebase development.

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:29.290
Sometimes you have a
checklist of the five things

00:36:29.290 --> 00:36:31.600
you know you need to do,
and then all of a sudden you

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:33.640
finish them, and you
realize that, hey, wait,

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:37.420
my application is
completely done now.

00:36:37.420 --> 00:36:39.700
But I guess we should
verify that for sure.

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:40.835
Let's just give it a run.

00:36:46.060 --> 00:36:47.700
MIKE MCDONALD: Hello world.

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.690
We're really unoriginal.

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:50.690
JEN TONG: There it goes.

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:53.765
We see the metadata
from the upload appear.

00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:57.440
Another transcript.

00:36:57.440 --> 00:36:58.548
- Hawaiians.

00:36:58.548 --> 00:37:01.480
JEN TONG: Ah.

00:37:01.480 --> 00:37:04.323
MIKE MCDONALD: Sorry about that.

00:37:04.323 --> 00:37:06.448
JEN TONG: What language is
that in, that it talked?

00:37:06.448 --> 00:37:07.762
MIKE MCDONALD: I said, hello.

00:37:07.762 --> 00:37:08.949
Something about Hawaiians.

00:37:08.949 --> 00:37:09.490
JEN TONG: OK.

00:37:09.490 --> 00:37:10.449
It didn't quite get it.

00:37:10.449 --> 00:37:12.240
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN:
You said it in English,

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:13.510
you selected Japanese.

00:37:13.510 --> 00:37:14.260
JEN TONG: Oh, this
is what happens

00:37:14.260 --> 00:37:15.640
when you don't use it right.

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:17.267
It was user error.

00:37:17.267 --> 00:37:18.850
Hawaiians, apparently,
is what happens

00:37:18.850 --> 00:37:20.558
when you tell it you're
speaking Japanese

00:37:20.558 --> 00:37:22.150
and you say hello world.

00:37:22.150 --> 00:37:23.762
Today we learned something.

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:30.010
So I guess our application
is done, then, right?

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:34.090
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Hold on.

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:36.232
Did we ever test it?

00:37:36.232 --> 00:37:37.690
JEN TONG: We tested
the full cycle.

00:37:37.690 --> 00:37:38.530
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN:
Yeah, so clearly

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:41.640
if we say we speak Japanese, and
we say hello world in English,

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:43.390
it doesn't really work.

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:44.080
JEN TONG: OK.

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:46.163
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: So we
probably should test it.

00:37:46.163 --> 00:37:49.899
But I have a good idea
for that, I think.

00:37:49.899 --> 00:37:50.690
JEN TONG: Oh, yeah.

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:53.950
This is what we learned.

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:56.620
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Let
me take this one, Jen.

00:37:56.620 --> 00:37:58.420
Can we switch back to
slides for a moment?

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:05.040
This one.

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:06.990
So when you're done
writing your functions,

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:09.180
you deploy them with
a single command.

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:11.910
You run Firebase deploy
and it uploads your code

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:16.280
to Google Cloud, where it runs
in Google Cloud functions.

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:18.520
It's a single command, but
it takes a bit of time.

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:20.990
So we've prepared it
this time for you.

00:38:20.990 --> 00:38:25.440
Now to be honest, I'll
admit that I came up

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:27.370
with that I wanted these apps.

00:38:27.370 --> 00:38:28.950
But when we started
this project,

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:30.570
I was sort of skeptical.

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:33.360
Because I wasn't really sure
that we could build this,

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:35.190
these two apps, in 30 minutes.

00:38:35.190 --> 00:38:37.050
And I'm watching the clock.

00:38:37.050 --> 00:38:38.550
They did.

00:38:38.550 --> 00:38:40.750
And that's actually
pretty amazing.

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:41.460
So--

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:46.960
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:48.870
But if you watch very
carefully, you'll

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:51.150
see that we actually did
slightly more than just two

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:51.700
apps.

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:54.050
There's a URL at the
bottom of the screen.

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:56.980
I'll make it a bit bigger.

00:38:56.980 --> 00:38:59.980
So we built a third
app, a web version

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:01.120
of the same application.

00:39:01.120 --> 00:39:03.010
And this is audio participation.

00:39:03.010 --> 00:39:07.030
Audience participation, and
audio participation, actually.

00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:09.220
So if you now go on your
phone or your laptop

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:13.520
to babelfire.cloud, I love
to use top-level domains.

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:16.180
You will see an app
very similar to ours.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.030
It's a read-only version of that
same app, because to be honest,

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:22.430
I didn't want to open up
an audio channel to all 300

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:23.770
of you.

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:26.170
And so it's a read only
version of the same app.

00:39:26.170 --> 00:39:29.680
You get a drop down box,
and you select the language.

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:31.660
And then you will
hear the last sentence

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:34.430
we said in the
language you select.

00:39:34.430 --> 00:39:37.570
So right now, that is the
Japanese interpretation

00:39:37.570 --> 00:39:39.730
of Mike saying hello world.

00:39:39.730 --> 00:39:41.980
Fairly interesting.

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:44.740
I have the same Android
app installed on my phone.

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:46.530
So I'm going to start it.

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:48.220
And I wonder--

00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:50.360
I was scanning for
familiar faces.

00:39:50.360 --> 00:39:53.830
Who here understands Dutch?

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:55.330
So I see a few hands going up.

00:39:55.330 --> 00:39:56.890
But actually that's
quite surprising,

00:39:56.890 --> 00:39:58.660
because you might
not realize it,

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:01.180
but if you open
[INAUDIBLE] on your phone,

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:03.280
you understand Dutch.

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:05.320
And you might not
have noticed, but I

00:40:05.320 --> 00:40:07.300
have a tiny bit of an
accent, because I'm not

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:08.620
a native English speaker.

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:11.110
I'm from the Netherlands,
and I speak Dutch.

00:40:11.110 --> 00:40:16.810
So I have the app open, and
I'm going to say Nederlands.

00:40:16.810 --> 00:40:18.310
And then I'm going
to say something,

00:40:18.310 --> 00:40:20.650
and you will hear it in the
language that you selected.

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:22.441
You won't understand
a word of what I said,

00:40:22.441 --> 00:40:25.060
but about five to 10
seconds later, you will.

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:33.830
[SPEAKING DUTCH]

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:36.670
I still hear a lot of Hawaiians,
so let's see how this works.

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:39.850
So my text is now being
uploaded to the cloud storage,

00:40:39.850 --> 00:40:42.950
it's being transcribed
into Dutch text,

00:40:42.950 --> 00:40:44.950
and is being translated
into the seven languages

00:40:44.950 --> 00:40:46.550
that we have
available in the app.

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:48.220
Then it's being
sent to your phone.

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:52.009
- The application works
and you understand Dutch.

00:40:52.009 --> 00:40:53.425
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN:
Did that work?

00:40:53.425 --> 00:41:01.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:01.360 --> 00:41:02.860
Demos are scary.

00:41:02.860 --> 00:41:04.720
But we're Firebase.

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:06.160
We like taking a challenge.

00:41:06.160 --> 00:41:09.130
So we could still have
faked this one, right?

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:11.230
I sort of knew what
I was going to say.

00:41:11.230 --> 00:41:14.980
So when you all were coming
in, we asked a few people

00:41:14.980 --> 00:41:18.214
if they speak a language from
the seven that we had selected.

00:41:18.214 --> 00:41:20.380
And I think there was
somebody on the first row that

00:41:20.380 --> 00:41:21.340
speaks Hindi.

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:22.630
Is that correct?

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:24.890
Can you come up to the
stage for a moment?

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:27.060
We'll do it right here.

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:27.940
OK.

00:41:27.940 --> 00:41:29.919
So we're going to do
a bit of a game here.

00:41:29.919 --> 00:41:30.460
Are you game?

00:41:30.460 --> 00:41:31.100
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:31.810
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Cool.

00:41:31.810 --> 00:41:33.400
So I'm going to
select Hindi there.

00:41:33.400 --> 00:41:35.590
Now I want you to
think of a short phrase

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:37.630
that you are going
to say to the app.

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:39.890
Now this is taking
a lot of risk,

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:42.240
because I don't understand
a word of Hindi.

00:41:45.670 --> 00:41:46.310
OK.

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:47.060
Let's see.

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:49.000
That's this option, right?

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:50.420
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:51.676
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Ready.

00:41:51.676 --> 00:41:56.113
COMPUTER VOICE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:56.113 --> 00:42:00.292
AUDIENCE: [SPEAKING HINDI]

00:42:00.292 --> 00:42:02.250
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Did
anyone understand that?

00:42:02.250 --> 00:42:03.870
Because I sure didn't.

00:42:03.870 --> 00:42:04.800
So one person there.

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:06.070
Was it friendly?

00:42:06.070 --> 00:42:09.690
We can still unplug the audio.

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:12.390
Let's see what
Translate makes of this.

00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:22.065
COMPUTER VOICE: You act
like you understand and.

00:42:22.065 --> 00:42:23.940
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Is
that is what you said?

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:25.550
Otherwise we're
going to try again.

00:42:25.550 --> 00:42:26.050
No?

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:26.550
OK.

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:27.280
Let's try again.

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:29.720
Once more.

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:30.770
No, it should be fine.

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:32.120
That is the correct one, right?

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:35.172
Let's try slightly louder, then.

00:42:35.172 --> 00:42:40.369
AUDIENCE: [SPEAKING HINDI]

00:42:40.369 --> 00:42:42.410
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: Fingers
crossed for this one.

00:42:42.410 --> 00:42:43.160
It's uploading.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:45.230
It's being
transcribed, translated

00:42:45.230 --> 00:42:46.892
to all seven
languages, one of which

00:42:46.892 --> 00:42:48.100
we've selected on the laptop.

00:42:48.100 --> 00:42:49.610
COMPUTER VOICE:
Today is Thursday.

00:42:49.610 --> 00:42:50.720
FRANK VAN PUFFELEN: That
sounds a lot better.

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:51.947
Thank you very much.

00:42:51.947 --> 00:42:55.286
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:57.680 --> 00:42:58.490
What do you know?

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:00.960
I do understand Hindi after all.

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:01.460
OK.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:04.410
Let's take this home.

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:06.620
So I started off with
painting a picture

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:08.960
of how I want apps to look.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:11.210
I want a universal
translation app

00:43:11.210 --> 00:43:15.100
that I call babel fire
because I'm a geek.

00:43:15.100 --> 00:43:19.890
And I said I wanted
two apps in 30 minutes.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:21.750
Then Mike came on
stage and explained

00:43:21.750 --> 00:43:23.958
how you would probably build
that app if you've never

00:43:23.958 --> 00:43:25.130
used Firebase.

00:43:25.130 --> 00:43:26.880
That's write the
application code,

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:29.400
write middleware, and then
interact with Google Cloud

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:30.880
Services from that middleware.

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:32.130
And Mike said, no, no, no, no.

00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:35.570
There is a better way to do
this, because there's Firebase.

00:43:35.570 --> 00:43:37.770
And with Firebase,
your application code

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:40.470
can communicate directly
with our powerful managed

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:42.000
backend services.

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:45.060
That means that we take
care of all the scalability

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:46.980
and security so
that you can focus

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:49.670
on building the functionality
that your users love.

00:43:49.670 --> 00:43:54.150
And in this case, I'm the
user and I love translations.

00:43:54.150 --> 00:43:57.520
So Mike wrote an app
that record an audio file

00:43:57.520 --> 00:44:01.280
and write it to cloud storage
through the Firebase SDK.

00:44:01.280 --> 00:44:04.630
And then he wrote metadata
about the audio file

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:06.730
into the Firebase database.

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:08.140
Then Jen came on stage.

00:44:08.140 --> 00:44:12.060
And [INAUDIBLE]
cloud functions that

00:44:12.060 --> 00:44:14.750
would be triggered
from the metadata

00:44:14.750 --> 00:44:17.780
and would take the audio file
and send it to the Cloud Speech

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:23.020
API, and return the transcript
of the text that was spoken.

00:44:23.020 --> 00:44:25.030
Then we write that
back into the database.

00:44:25.030 --> 00:44:27.040
We have another
cloud function that

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:29.965
gets triggered that sends
the text to Cloud Translate

00:44:29.965 --> 00:44:32.050
and translates it into
the seven languages

00:44:32.050 --> 00:44:34.282
that we have
available in our app.

00:44:34.282 --> 00:44:36.240
We get the translations
back, and we wrote them

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:38.940
into the database, where Mike
picked them up in the app

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:43.220
and spoke them on the device
with the text to speech.

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:45.920
This all in 30 minutes.

00:44:45.920 --> 00:44:47.510
Sure, we did some prep work.

00:44:47.510 --> 00:44:49.401
But to be honest,
I doubt there's

00:44:49.401 --> 00:44:50.900
a lot of technologies
out there that

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:53.340
allow you to do that
much in this little time.

00:44:56.110 --> 00:44:57.290
And this is Google.

00:44:57.290 --> 00:45:00.080
So this has all been
built to Google scale.

00:45:00.080 --> 00:45:02.470
That means that if
your app takes off,

00:45:02.470 --> 00:45:04.510
and you suddenly
are indeed implanted

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:08.220
into every human in the
world, it will scale with you.

00:45:08.220 --> 00:45:10.110
We also have a lot more APIs.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:12.220
We just used two Cloud APIs.

00:45:12.220 --> 00:45:14.904
There's 150 or more of those.

00:45:14.904 --> 00:45:16.320
And this is one
of the things that

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:18.750
gets us excited about cloud
functions in combination

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:22.500
with Firebase, because
we just picked two APIs

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:25.540
and made a very cool
story out of it.

00:45:25.540 --> 00:45:29.020
And you can pick other APIs and
build your own story out of it.

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:31.230
In fact, if you like
doing that, then I highly

00:45:31.230 --> 00:45:34.170
recommend that you stay in
this room after our session.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:36.540
Because we have Brendan
and Thomas coming up here.

00:45:36.540 --> 00:45:39.510
They have an entire box full
of these snippets of codes.

00:45:39.510 --> 00:45:41.760
And they're just going to
glue them together and build

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:43.169
beautiful stories out of them.

00:45:43.169 --> 00:45:45.210
I highly recommend that
you stay around for that.

00:45:45.210 --> 00:45:45.990
I definitely will.

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:54.100
OK, and with that, I think
we're ready to wrap up here.

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:56.500
So I really want to
thank you for being here

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:59.500
and building this
application with us.

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:01.960
If you have any
questions about Firebase,

00:46:01.960 --> 00:46:04.120
come meet us in
the experts area,

00:46:04.120 --> 00:46:06.910
which I think is downstairs,
if I remember correctly.

00:46:06.910 --> 00:46:09.730
Or to be honest, find anyone
with a bright yellow shirt

00:46:09.730 --> 00:46:11.060
with Firebase on it.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:14.034
In fact, I see the two founders
of the company sitting there.

00:46:14.034 --> 00:46:15.700
They will love to
answer your questions,

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:18.241
because there's-- to be honest--
nothing more that Firebasers

00:46:18.241 --> 00:46:20.830
love more than interacting
with developers that use

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:21.757
our technology.

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:24.935
With that, I want to thank you.

00:46:24.935 --> 00:46:27.060
And I look forward to
actually tethering to as many

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:29.070
of you as possible
during the rest of Next,

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:31.485
because I can't wait to
see what you build next.

00:46:31.485 --> 00:46:40.759
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:40.759 --> 00:46:42.300
Mike says we have
time for questions.

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:43.190
Anyone?

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:44.320
MIKE MCDONALD: Come
up to the microphones

